-- title:
--   Kernel of u₀ from proportional Cartier tangents
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$ and a ring map $\iota \colon W(\mathbb F_{p^2}) \to W(k)$, and let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $W(k)/pW(k)$ whose Cartier module splits, in the sense that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces for $j = \iota$ followed by reduction are complementary (`hcΦ`), together with an additive map $r_\Phi \colon \mathbb Z_p^2 \to$ the $N$-module of the resulting graded Cartier data. Let $\kappa$ be a field of characteristic $p$ which is a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra, $\psi \colon W(k) \to \kappa$ a ring map, $t = (X, n, \rho)$ a rigidified object over $\kappa$ relative to $\Phi$, and $Q$ a Drinfeld datum over $\kappa$ for $\mathcal O = \mathbb Z_p$, $K = \mathbb Q_p$, $\pi = p$, consisting of full lattices $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ in $\mathbb Q_p^2$ varying over $\operatorname{Spec} \kappa$, invertible modules $T_0, T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to $p$, and stalk maps $u_0, u_1$. Assume `hQ`, that $Q$ is a Cartier quadruple for $t$: $\rho$ is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism from $\bar\Phi_\psi$ to $\bar X$, and there are isomorphisms of $Q.T_0$, $Q.T_1$ with the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ Lie submodules of $X$ intertwining $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ with the Verschiebung-type operator `lieVarpi`, compatibly, at every point, with the description of $N_0$, $N_1$ by eta-sections and with $u_0$, $u_1$; assume also `hOD`, that $\rho$ is such an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism. Fix a point $x \in \operatorname{Spec}\kappa$ and $f \in \kappa$ with $f \notin x$, such that over the localisation away from $f$ the Cartier modules of $X_f$, $\bar X_f$ and $\bar\Phi_f$ are graded (the three `IsCompl` hypotheses `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦg`), and let $L$ be a canonical Cartier $L$-map on the Cartier module $M$ of $X_f$. Let $e$ be a $\mathbb Z_p$-basis $(e_0, e_1)$ of $N_0(x)$, and let $z_0, z_1$ in the $N$-module of $X_f$ be eta-sections in degree $0$ for the vectors $e_0, e_1 \in \mathbb Q_p^2$: each lies in the intersection of $\eta(L)$ with the degree-$0$ piece, and its reduction to $\bar X_f$ is related to $r_\Phi$ by the lattice relation of exponent $t.n$. Let $m_0, m_1 \in M$ have images in $M/VM$ equal to the elements $u(L)$ attaches to $z_0, z_1$. Suppose finally that for some $c \in \kappa$ one has $\operatorname{tangent}(m_1)_j = -c\,\operatorname{tangent}(m_0)_j$ in $\kappa_f$ for both $j$, and $\operatorname{tangent}(m_0)_j \neq 0$ for some $j$. Then the kernel of $u_0(x) \colon \mathcal O_{\kappa,x} \otimes_{\mathbb Z_p} N_0(x) \to (T_0)_x$ is the $\mathcal O_{\kappa,x}$-submodule generated by the single element $c \otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$.
--
--   This is the local computation identifying, at a point of the base, the line killed by the degree-$0$ stalk map of a Cartier quadruple in terms of the tangent vectors of Cartier-module representatives of two eta-sections: proportionality of the tangents with factor $-c$ pins the kernel down to the line spanned by $c \otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$. It is the common core used in the node case and in both branch cases of the recognition of the Drinfeld period point for the explicit families of special formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, and is cited by the three corresponding statements identifying $\ker u_0$ for edge and node families.
